**Author:** Robisson Oliveira - Sr. Cloud Application Architect at AWS
<br/>

### _This approach is still in development, but I'd like to ask for feedback from the community as I make progress on the rest._

The goal of this RFC is to propose a design to enable [the SAAS Builder Toolkit (SBT](https://github.com/awslabs/sbt-aws) to support [cell-based architecture](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/reducing-scope-of-impact-with-cell-based-architecture/what-is-a-cell-based-architecture.html). This kind of design means for SBT some changes for its deployment, observability and possible cross-account communication model.

When asked to think of what goes into building a Software-as-a-service (SaaS) app, some might think about only the value that the SaaS app provides. For instance, they may consider the messaging component of Slack, or maybe the ability to sketch and save drawings in Lucidchart. What is sometimes not considered are the tools, services and infrastructure required to smoothly operate the app. The reality is, building a SaaS app requires both, and at AWS, we've observed a recurring pattern among successful SaaS implementations — the separation of concerns into two distinct planes: the Application Plane and the Control Plane.

The application plane, as described above, is where your customers go to realize value. This is where the core functionality of your SaaS offering resides — the business logic, services, and unique value proposition that you deliver to your customers, or tenants. This is the heart of your application, where you pour your innovation and creativity to solve real-world problems.

On the other hand, the control plane is responsible for providing tools to help you manage and operate your tenants through a single, unified experience. It's operating, behind the scenes, ensuring that the service you're offering is performing at the desired level. The control plane is foundational to any multi-tenant SaaS model, providing a collection of shared services that orchestrate the multi-tenant experience for your application.

Below we have the main building blocks of the SBT:

![image (1)](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/cb94fd25-e20c-49e9-b83f-8ee99efe96b8)

For some customers, building large SAAS applications with high SLAs is not enough.

SBT allows our customers a flexible environment to allocate their own customers in their SAAS environment in their application plans, whether in the SILO model, where each customer has a dedicated infrastructure, or in the POOL model, where multiple customers share the same infrastructure. A customer in this context is a tenant within the SBT taxonomy.

SBT's current capabilities place our customers in a binary division of operation/deployment model, SILO or POOL. For large-scale applications, this may not be enough, as an application may have thousands or millions of customers within 1 POOL, or even in multiple regions. Thus, an incident or outage within 1 customer's POOL still has a major impact on their business and the experience of their customers.

This is where cell-based architecture can bring more flexibility to this need. By supporting a cell-based architecture, SBT can give customers a simplified way to operate their applications in a more predictable and scalable way, by grouping their SILO or POOL tenants into cells (but this grouping will be discussed later in this document), thus allowing their applications to reduce the impact of their failures, in addition to having more scalability and testability for their production environments.

## Current SBT deployment capabilities


<img width="1035" alt="image (2)" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/5260bde4-51df-4cd5-880d-fcdb58cf0677" />


## Design for cell-based architecture in SBT

The topics for discussing in this document in the process of cell-based architecture are:

* [Control plane and data plane](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/reducing-scope-of-impact-with-cell-based-architecture/control-plane-and-data-plane.html) - already covered by SBT
* [Cell design](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/reducing-scope-of-impact-with-cell-based-architecture/cell-design.html)
* [Cell partition](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/reducing-scope-of-impact-with-cell-based-architecture/cell-partition.html)
* [Cell routing](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/reducing-scope-of-impact-with-cell-based-architecture/cell-routing.html)
* Cell boundaries
* [Cell sizing](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/reducing-scope-of-impact-with-cell-based-architecture/cell-sizing.html)
* [Cell placement](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/reducing-scope-of-impact-with-cell-based-architecture/cell-placement.html)
* [Cell migration](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/reducing-scope-of-impact-with-cell-based-architecture/cell-migration.html)
* [Cell deployment](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/reducing-scope-of-impact-with-cell-based-architecture/cell-deployment.html)
* [Cell observability](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/reducing-scope-of-impact-with-cell-based-architecture/cell-observability.html)


Since SBT already supports many of the principles advocated by a cell-based architecture, we will describe in this document the pros and cons of some approaches in an evolutionary way. Such as:

* Is it really necessary to add the cell as a new taxonomy item to SBT?
* Support for multiples POOLs at the same time
* Support for different cell-boundaries
* Support for same tier, but different cells/pools
* Support a single plane of glass for observability
* Support gradual deployment between cells/pools

## Is it really necessary to add the cell as a new taxonomy item to SBT?

In the context of cell-based architecture, a cell is a copy or a complete deployment of an application, running independently. The sbt application plane can currently also be run in this way. Although sbt currently deploys the control plane and application plane in the same account, they do not communicate directly, but rather via events through AWS EventBridge.

This allows the control plane and application plane to be in any AWS account or region and still communicate, with the appropriate cross-account configurations only in EventBridge.

Another detail is that sbt extends its constructs/stacks from the CDK classes themselves, so we already have in the property settings the env: {accountID, region} which allows us to configure our stacks wherever we want.

Taking advantage of this type of configuration, we could further decouple SBT to be multi-account without making changes to the project, allowing for the following scenarios:

* Tenants per account, in the SILO or POOL model
* Cell-based architecture, where each application plane in an account can be considered a cell. In this case, a cell router would still be required depending on the SAAS delivery model

## Support for different cell-boundaries

Considering what was discussed in "Is it really necessary to add the cell as a new taxonomy item to SBT?" we can do cell-based architecture using SBT and create independent deployments of a SAAS application having multiple application planes (here being cells) communicating with the same control plane. It could even be possible to reduce the blast radius of the control plane itself by making it cell-based, but that will not be covered in this document.

The diagram below shows a design where we can have 1 or more tenants per cell in the SILO model or pool in 1 or more AWS Accounts. These variations can be useful because each business has particular needs in managing its tenants. Even a business that decides to have its customers in dedicated tiers, for example, may come close to the AWS account limits and it may be interesting to use cell-based to group dedicated tenants up to a number X in a cell and up to respectively AWS account.


<img width="990" alt="image (3)" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/c51f410f-8455-4b3c-bf92-d1d5fa175bb7" />


In practice, this can be done using the constructs that SBT already offers today. SBT's basic communication is to trigger events from the control plane to the application through EventBridge rules.


<img width="2079" alt="image (4)" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/e7a36d1e-33e9-4a59-b948-3407b32cc181" />


The current SBT approach for a single account is to instantiate the control plane and pass its eventManager (EventBridge) to the application plane. To enable the multi-account scenario we only need a second EventBridge to receive events from the control plane.

To enable the above drawing, we can have the following configuration (still under validation) for the control plane. We are instantiating the Control plane normally, but just passing a CustomEventManager, to be able to enable cross-account communication.

To make things easier, we created a configuration file to establish how many cells we want and in which accounts in a json file, which the CDK stack will use to provision the application plane cells.

```json
{
    "cells": [
      {
        "id": "cell-01",
        "name": "cell-01",
        "totalCapacity": 100,
        "currentCapacity": 0,
        "tier": "basic",
        "topology": "POOLS_PER_CELL",
        "pollName": "default-pool",
        "account": "XXXXXXXXXXX",
        "region": "us-east-1"
      },
      {
        "id": "cell-02",
        "name": "cell-02",
        "totalCapacity": 100,
        "currentCapacity": 0,
        "tier": "basic",
        "topology": "SILOR_POOLS_PER_CELL",
        "pollName": "default-pool",
        "account": "XXXXXXXXXXX",
        "region": "us-east-1"
      }
    ]
  }
```

The convention created here supports three deployment topology models:

1. **POOLS_PER_CELL** - One or more pools per cell where each pool can host multiple tenants
2. **SILOS_PER_CELL** - One or more silos per cell where each silo hosts exactly one tenant
3. **SILOS_POOLS_PER_CELL** - Hybrid model supporting both pools and silos within the same cell

Then, we need to boostrap the CDK to provision services cross-account. In this examples we are enable control plane account todo all the work, but in production environments would be a good ideia to have a dedicated account just for monitoring tools and pipelines,

```bash
#!/bin/sh

cdk bootstrap aws://${CONTROL_PLANE_ACCOUNT_ID}/${AWS_REGION}

cdk bootstrap aws://${APPLICATION_PLANE_ACCOUNT_ID}/${AWS_REGION} \
    --trust ${CONTROL_PLANE_ACCOUNT_ID} \
    --cloudformation-execution-policies "arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/${YOUR_SCOPE_ROLE}"
```


```typescript
//control-plane-stack.ts
import { Construct } from 'constructs';
import { CustomEventManager } from './control- plane/custom-event-manager';
import { CognitoAuth, ControlPlane, DetailType, IEventManager } from '@cdklabs/sbt-aws';
import { RemovalPolicy, Stack, StackProps } from 'aws-cdk-lib';
import { CellManagementService, CellManagementTable } from './control- plane/cell-management';
import * as targets from 'aws-cdk-lib/aws-events-targets';
import { ICell } from './control- plane/cell-management/cell-configuration-interface';

//create Control PlaneProps
interface ControlPlaneProps extends StackProps {
  env: { account: string, region: string };
  CellAccounts: string[];
  systemAdminEmail: string;
  cells: ICell[]
}

export class ControlPlaneStack extends Stack {
  public readonly regApiUrl: string;
  public readonly eventManager: IEventManager;
  public readonly eventBusArn: string;
  public readonly controlPlaneUrl: string;
  public readonly clientId: string;
  public readonly authorizationServer: string;
  public readonly wellKnownEndpointUrl: string;
  public readonly table: CellManagementTable;

  constructor(scope: Construct, id: string, props: ControlPlaneProps) {
    super(scope, id, props);
    const version = 'v2';
    const eventManager = new CustomEventManager(
      this, 'EventManager',
      {
        trustedAccounts: props.CellAccounts,
        suffix: `-${version}`
      }
    );

    eventManager.eventBus.applyRemovalPolicy(RemovalPolicy.DESTROY);

    const auth = new CognitoAuth(this, 'Auth', {
      setAPIGWScopes: false
    });

    const controlPlane = new ControlPlane(this, 'ControlPlane', {
      auth,
      systemAdminEmail: props.systemAdminEmail,
      eventManager: eventManager
    });

    const cellManagementService = new CellManagementService(this, 'cellManagementService', {
      auth,
      eventManager,
      cells: props.cells
    });

    eventManager.addTargetToEvent(
      this,
      {
        eventType: DetailType.ONBOARDING_REQUEST,
        target: new targets.LambdaFunction(cellManagementService.lambda)
      }
    )

    this.table = cellManagementService.table
    this.regApiUrl = controlPlane.controlPlaneAPIGatewayUrl;
    this.eventManager = controlPlane.eventManager;
    this.controlPlaneUrl = controlPlane.controlPlaneAPIGatewayUrl;
    this.eventBusArn = controlPlane.eventManager.busArn;
    this.clientId = auth.userClientId;
    this.wellKnownEndpointUrl = auth.wellKnownEndpointUrl;
    const tokenEndpoint = auth.tokenEndpoint;
    this.authorizationServer = tokenEndpoint.substring(0, tokenEndpoint.indexOf('/oauth2/token'));
  }
}

```
The CustomEventManager has just the configuration needed to allow the cross-account communication of EventBridge. As the sbt EventManager currently does not support extending its events, a workaround was necessary within the EventManager itself, which was created custom, adding events with direct access to the supportedEvents property.
<br/>
Here we have an important whole for SBT, not only to facilitate multi-account and cell-based integration, but also to encourage sbt API clients to create their own events extending the platform.

```typescript
//custom-event-manager.ts
import * as sbt from '@cdklabs/sbt-aws';
import * as events from 'aws-cdk-lib/aws-events';
import { Stack } from 'aws-cdk-lib';
import { Construct } from 'constructs';

export interface CustomEventManagerProps {
  trustedAccounts?: string[];
  suffix?: string;
}

export class CustomEventManager extends sbt.EventManager {
  constructor(scope: Construct, id: string, props: CustomEventManagerProps) {

    const stack = Stack.of(scope);
    // Create the event bus first
    const eventBus = new events.EventBus(scope, `event-bus-${props?.suffix || ''}`, {
      eventBusName: `${stack.stackName}-local-event-bus${props?.suffix || ''}`
    });

    // Pass the event bus to the parent class
    super(scope, id, {
      eventBus: eventBus
    });

    // add nee events to EventFlow
    this.supportedEvents.onboardingCellRequest = this.controlPlaneEventSource;
    this.supportedEvents.onboardingCellSuccess = this.controlPlaneEventSource;
    this.supportedEvents.onboardingCellFailure = this.controlPlaneEventSource;
    this.supportedEvents.offboardingCellRequest = this.controlPlaneEventSource;
    this.supportedEvents.offboardingCellSuccess = this.controlPlaneEventSource;
    this.supportedEvents.offboardingCellFailure = this.controlPlaneEventSource;
    
    // Add cross-account permissions if trustedAccounts are provided
    if (props?.trustedAccounts && props.trustedAccounts.length > 0) {
      props.trustedAccounts.forEach((accountId, index) => {
        const statementId = `AllowAccount${props?.suffix || ''}-${accountId}`;

        new events.CfnEventBusPolicy(scope, `EventBusPolicy-${index}`, {
          eventBusName: eventBus.eventBusName,
          statementId: statementId,
          statement: {
            Effect: 'Allow',
            Principal: {
              AWS: `arn:aws:iam::${accountId}:root`
            },
            Action: [
              'events:*',
            ],
            Resource: [
              `arn:aws:events:${stack.region}:${stack.account}:rule/${this.busName}/*`
            ]
          }
        });
      });
    }
  }
}
```
Our application will receive this EventManager from the control plane and create another EventManager that will live in the application plane:

```typescript
// cell-stack.ts
import * as events from 'aws-cdk-lib/aws-events';
import * as targets from 'aws-cdk-lib/aws-events-targets';
import { Construct } from 'constructs';
import { CustomEventManager } from './control- plane/custom-event-manager';
import { Effect, PolicyDocument, PolicyStatement } from 'aws-cdk-lib/aws-iam';
import { CoreApplicationPlane, IEventManager, ScriptJob, ScriptJobProps } from '@cdklabs/sbt-aws';
import { RemovalPolicy, Stack, StackProps } from 'aws-cdk-lib';
import { Queue } from 'aws-cdk-lib/aws-sqs';
import { CellManagementService, CellManagementTable } from './control- plane/cell-management';
import * as fs from 'fs';
import { ServicesStack } from './application-plane/services-stack';
import { ControlPlaneStack } from './control-plane-stack';
import { ICell } from './control- plane/cell-management/cell-configuration-interface';

interface CellProps extends StackProps {
  eventManager: IEventManager;
  env: { account: string, region: string };
  controlPlaneAccount: string;
  controlPlaneStack: ControlPlaneStack;
  table: CellManagementTable;
  cell: ICell
}

export class CellStack extends Stack {
  constructor(scope: Construct, id: string, props: CellProps) {
    super(scope, id, props);

    const cellEventManager = new CustomEventManager(
      this, 'EventManager',
      {
        trustedAccounts: [props.controlPlaneAccount],
        suffix: `-${props.cell.name}`
      }
    );

    cellEventManager.eventBus.applyRemovalPolicy(RemovalPolicy.DESTROY);

    // to create an AMazon SQS Queue called cell-based and add as a target to cellEventManager
    const sqsQueue = new Queue(this, 'cell-based', {
      queueName: 'cell-based',
    });

    cellEventManager.addTargetToEvent(this, {
      eventType: CellManagementService.ONBOARDING_CELL_REQUEST,
      target: new targets.SqsQueue(sqsQueue),
    });

    // Create the rule with explicit reference to the event bus
    new events.Rule(this, `EventRule-${props.cell.name}`, {
      eventBus: events.EventBus.fromEventBusName(this, `ImportedEventBus-${props.cell.name}`, props.eventManager.busArn
      ),
      eventPattern: {
        source: [props.eventManager.controlPlaneEventSource],
        detailType: [CellManagementService.ONBOARDING_CELL_REQUEST],
        detail: {
          cellID: [props.cell.name],
        },
      },
      targets: [new targets.EventBus(cellEventManager.eventBus)]
    });

    new ServicesStack(this, 'Services', {
      env: props.env,
      appSiteDistributionId: "sitesStack.applicationSiteDistribution.distributionId",
      appSiteCloudFrontDomain: "sitesStack.applicationSiteDistribution.distributionDomainName",
      cell: props.cell,
      table: props.table,
      controlPlaneStack: props.controlPlaneStack
    });

    const provisioningScriptJobProps: ScriptJobProps = {
      permissions: new PolicyDocument({
        statements: [
          new PolicyStatement({
            actions: ['*'],
            resources: ['*'],
            effect: Effect.ALLOW,
          }),
        ],
      }),
      script: fs.readFileSync('./scripts/provisioning.sh', 'utf8'),
      environmentStringVariablesFromIncomingEvent: [
        'tenantId',
        'cellID',
        'tier',
        'tenantName',
        'email',
        'tenantStatus',
        "deploymentId",
        "deploymentName",
        "deploymentType",
        "registrationStatus",
        "tenantRegistrationId",
        "status",
        "createdAt"
      ],
      environmentVariablesToOutgoingEvent: {
        tenantData: [
          'tenantS3Bucket',
          'tenantConfig',
          'someOtherVariable',
          'tenantStatus',
          'tenantId',
          'tier',
          'cellID',
          'tenantName',
          'deploymentName',
          'email',
          'deploymentId',
          'deploymentType',
          'registrationStatus',
          'tenantRegistrationId',
          'status',
          'createdAt'
        ],
        tenantRegistrationData: ['registrationStatus'],
      },
      scriptEnvironmentVariables: {
        TEST: 'test',
      },
      eventManager: cellEventManager,
      // Add missing required properties
      jobIdentifierKey: 'cellID',
      jobFailureStatus: { 'jobID': "$jobID" },
      incomingEvent: CellManagementService.ONBOARDING_CELL_REQUEST,
      outgoingEvent: {
        success: CellManagementService.ONBOARDING_CELL_SUCCESS,
        failure: CellManagementService.ONBOARDING_CELL_FAILURE,
      }
    };

    const deprovisioningScriptJobProps: ScriptJobProps = {
      permissions: new PolicyDocument({
        statements: [
          new PolicyStatement({
            actions: [
              'cloudformation:DeleteStack',
              'cloudformation:DescribeStacks',
              's3:DeleteBucket',
            ],
            resources: ['*'],
            effect: Effect.ALLOW,
          }),
        ],
      }),
      script: `
        echo "starting..."
        echo "tenantId: $tenantId"
        echo "done!"
      `,
      environmentStringVariablesFromIncomingEvent: ['tenantId'],
      environmentVariablesToOutgoingEvent: {
        tenantRegistrationData: ['registrationStatus'],
      },
      eventManager: cellEventManager,
      // Add missing required properties
      jobIdentifierKey: 'cellID',
      jobFailureStatus: { 'jobID': "$jobID" },
      incomingEvent: CellManagementService.OFFBOARDING_CELL_REQUEST,
      outgoingEvent: {
        success: CellManagementService.OFFBOARDING_CELL_SUCCESS,
        failure: CellManagementService.OFFBOARDING_CELL_FAILURE,
      }
    };

    const provisioningJobScript: ScriptJob = new ScriptJob(
      this,
      'provisioningJobScript',
      provisioningScriptJobProps,
    );

    const deprovisioningJobScript: ScriptJob = new ScriptJob(
      this,
      'deprovisioningJobScript',
      deprovisioningScriptJobProps
    );

    new CoreApplicationPlane(this, 'CoreCell', {
      eventManager: cellEventManager,
      scriptJobs: [provisioningJobScript, deprovisioningJobScript],
    });


  }
}
```

In addition to this scenario, the way to provision the components that will reside within each cell/aws account will follow what was designed within the provisioning and deprovisioning scripts, as the sbt project already normally supports.

Now we need to add a service to the control plane onboarding flow to decide which cell/account a tenant will be provisioned for each time it is registered. As this can vary greatly from business to business, it was changed so that the DetailType.ONBOARDING_REQUEST event invokes this lambda called CellManagementService and this lambda will contain all the decision logic to understand in which cell a new tenant will be provisioned.

Here we can have the following considerations:
- We may have cells per tier, so the cell will be selected according to the tier.
- It may be that the cell of the tier at that time is full, so we plan to provision a new cell before provisioning the tenant. - It may be that the tier of this tenant uses a dedicated cell, so we plan to provision a new cell before provisioning the tenant.

A cell is the entire SAAS application, like a dedicated tenant with its own infrastructure, so each SAAS business may have its own decision on how to provision differently.

The CellManagementService lambda is responsible for responding to the onboardingCellRequest event and provisioning the lambda according to the data received. Below is the logic of the lambda that will search for or create POOLS or create or search for SILOS.

```typescript
//  cell-management.service.ts
import { Construct } from 'constructs';
import { CellManagementLambda } from './cell-management-funcs';
import { DetailType, IAuth, IEventManager } from '@cdklabs/sbt-aws';
import { CellManagementTable } from './cell-management.table';
import { Function } from 'aws-cdk-lib/aws-lambda';
import { ICell } from './cell-configuration-interface';

export interface CellManagementServiceProps {
  readonly auth: IAuth;
  readonly eventManager: IEventManager;
  cells: ICell[]
}

export class CellManagementService extends Construct {

  public static ONBOARDING_CELL_REQUEST = "onboardingCellRequest" as DetailType;
  public static ONBOARDING_CELL_SUCCESS = "onboardingCellSuccess" as DetailType;
  public static ONBOARDING_CELL_FAILURE = "onboardingCellFailure" as DetailType;
  public static OFFBOARDING_CELL_REQUEST = "offboardingCellRequest" as DetailType;
  public static OFFBOARDING_CELL_SUCCESS = "offboardingCellSuccess" as DetailType;
  public static OFFBOARDING_CELL_FAILURE = "offboardingCellFailure" as DetailType;

  public readonly table: CellManagementTable;
  public readonly lambda: Function;

  constructor(scope: Construct, id: string, props: CellManagementServiceProps) {
    super(scope, id);

    const table = new CellManagementTable(this, 'CellManagementTable', {
      cellsConfiguration: props.cells
    });
    const lambda = new CellManagementLambda(this, 'CellManagementLambda', {
      eventManager: props.eventManager,
      table,
    });

    this.table = table;
    this.lambda = lambda.CellManagementFunc;
  }
}
```

```typescript
//cell-management.table.ts
import * as iam from 'aws-cdk-lib/aws-iam';
import { addTemplateTag } from '@cdklabs/sbt-aws';
import { AttributeType, Table, BillingMode } from 'aws-cdk-lib/aws-dynamodb';
import { Construct } from 'constructs';
import { ICell } from './cell-configuration-interface';
import { AwsCustomResource, AwsCustomResourcePolicy, PhysicalResourceId } from 'aws-cdk-lib/custom-resources';
import { RemovalPolicy } from 'aws-cdk-lib';

interface CellManagementTableProps {
  cellsConfiguration: ICell[];
}

/**
 * Represents the tables for managing cells, deployments and tenants in the application.
 *
 * @class CellManagementTable
 * @extends Construct
 */
export class CellManagementTable extends Construct {
  /**
   * The table that stores the cells information.
   */
  public readonly cells: Table;

  /**
   * The table that stores the deployments (pools and silos) information.
   */
  public readonly deployments: Table;

  /**
   * The table that stores the tenants information.
   */
  public readonly tenants: Table;

  constructor(scope: Construct, id: string, props: CellManagementTableProps) {
    super(scope, id);
    addTemplateTag(this, 'Tables');

    // Create Cells table
    this.cells = new Table(this, 'Cells', {
      partitionKey: { name: 'cellID', type: AttributeType.STRING },
      billingMode: BillingMode.PAY_PER_REQUEST,
      tableName: 'cells',
      removalPolicy: RemovalPolicy.DESTROY
    });

    // Create Deployments table (for both pools and silos)
    this.deployments = new Table(this, 'Deployments', {
      partitionKey: { name: 'PK', type: AttributeType.STRING },
      sortKey: { name: 'SK', type: AttributeType.STRING },
      billingMode: BillingMode.PAY_PER_REQUEST,
      tableName: 'deployments',
      removalPolicy: RemovalPolicy.DESTROY
    });

    // Add GSI for querying deployments by cell
    this.deployments.addGlobalSecondaryIndex({
      indexName: 'GSI1',
      partitionKey: { name: 'GSI1PK', type: AttributeType.STRING },
      sortKey: { name: 'GSI1SK', type: AttributeType.STRING },
    });

    // Create Tenants table
    this.tenants = new Table(this, 'Tenants', {
      partitionKey: { name: 'PK', type: AttributeType.STRING },
      sortKey: { name: 'SK', type: AttributeType.STRING },
      billingMode: BillingMode.PAY_PER_REQUEST,
      tableName: 'tenants',
      removalPolicy: RemovalPolicy.DESTROY
    });

    // Add GSIs for tenants table
    this.tenants.addGlobalSecondaryIndex({
      indexName: 'GSI1',
      partitionKey: { name: 'GSI1PK', type: AttributeType.STRING },
      sortKey: { name: 'GSI1SK', type: AttributeType.STRING },
    });

    this.tenants.addGlobalSecondaryIndex({
      indexName: 'GSI2',
      partitionKey: { name: 'GSI2PK', type: AttributeType.STRING },
      sortKey: { name: 'GSI2SK', type: AttributeType.STRING },
    });

    // Seed initial cells configuration
    props.cellsConfiguration.forEach((cell) => {
      new AwsCustomResource(this, `CellEntryResource-${cell.id}`, {
        onCreate: {
          service: 'DynamoDB',
          action: 'putItem',
          parameters: {
            TableName: 'cells',
            Item: {
              cellID: { S: cell.id },
              name: { S: cell.name },
              region: { S: cell.region },
              account: { S: cell.account },
              totalCapacity: { N: cell.totalCapacity.toString() },
              currentCapacity: { N: cell.currentCapacity.toString() },
              topology: { S: cell.topology },
              tier: { S: cell.tier },
              status: { S: 'ACTIVE' }
            }
          },
          physicalResourceId: PhysicalResourceId.of(`CellEntry-${cell.id}`),
        },
        onDelete: {
          service: 'DynamoDB',
          action: 'deleteItem',
          parameters: {
            TableName: 'cells',
            Key: {
              cellID: { S: cell.id },
            },
          },
        },
        policy: AwsCustomResourcePolicy.fromStatements([
          new iam.PolicyStatement({
            effect: iam.Effect.ALLOW,
            actions: [
              'dynamodb:PutItem',
              'dynamodb:DeleteItem',
              'dynamodb:GetItem',
            ],
            resources: [this.cells.tableArn],
          }),
        ]),
      });
    });
  }
}
